      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML Certificate of Conformity for a water meter.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 9\n- Project number: SO16202611\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 14 July 2016\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant: KROHNE Altometer (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: Water meter\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Module designation: not applicable here (R49 doesn't use modules like MID) — I'll leave null or omit\n- Description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2.\" Plus the more detailed description on page 2: \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- R49 has parts 1, 2, 3 (2006 edition). The certificate number is R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01. The recommendation id should be canonical: \"R49\". Edition: 2006. The cert references R49-1/2006 (E), R49-2/2006 (E), R49-3/2006 (E).\n- accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] — the water meter is class 1 and 2 (per OIML R49 accuracy classes).\n\n**Test reports:**\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01: 41 pages, 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01: 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01: 40 pages, 4 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01: 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- NMi-13200159-01: 6 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-15200645-01: 21 pages, 4 annexes\n\nRole: type_evaluation_report for all.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Initial, 28 March 2012, \"-\" (or null)\n- 1, 22 October 2012, New software version added\n- 2, 16 April 2013, New software version added\n- 3, 26 September 2014, New software version added\n- 4, 18 March 2015, Added sizes\n- 5, 21 April 2015, New software version added\n- 6, 13 July 2015, New software version added\n- 7, 30 March 2016, New electronics, software version and optional power supply added\n- 8, 16 June 2016, New software version added\n- 9, 14 July 2016, New software version added\n\nRevision should be string per schema (\"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)\"). \"Initial\" for the first.\n\n**Characteristics (page 2):**\n- type: WATERFLUX 3070\n- Min/max admissible temperature (°C): 0,1/50 → hmm, \"0,1/50\" — this is European decimal: 0.1/50? Wait, \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C): 0,1/50\". That would be min 0.1°C? That seems odd for cold potable water (usually 0.1°C to 50°C for hot water meters per R49... actually MAT 0.1°C/50°C is a standard cold water combination: 0,1°C / 50°C means minimum admissible temperature 0.1°C, maximum 50°C). Yes, in R49, MAT/MAT combinations like 0,1°C/30°C, 0,1°C/50°C. So min 0.1, max 50, unit °C.\n- Maximum pressure: 16 bar(g) for DN200 and smaller; 10 bar(g) for DN250 and larger. This varies by meter size → config_level or model_level. Since it varies by size (configuration axis = meter size), I could put it in config_level with axis \"meter_size\" and conditions \"DN200 and smaller\" → 16 bar(g), \"DN250 and larger\" → 10 bar(g). Actually could also fold into matrix table rows. The main flow table already includes meter size. Hmm, the flow table on page 3 doesn't include pressure. I'll put maximum pressure in config_level with axis \"meter_size\".\n- Indicating range (m³): 99.999.999 — this is a large number using periods as thousand separators (99,999,999 m³). Value: 99999999. Also on page 4 the indicating range table: all sizes 99.999.999. So type_level: indicating_range = 99999999 m³.\n- Orientation: All positions → string value.\n- Environmental class: C → string.\n- Power supply:\n  - Type: Battery 3,6 V → value 3.6, unit V. Also external battery pack with output 3,6 V. Also FlexPower (optional): 10…30 V DC or 110…230 V AC / 50-60 Hz.\n  - Let me structure:\n    - power_supply_type: \"Battery\" — hmm. Actually let me create:\n    - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n    - external_battery_pack_output_voltage: 3.6 V\n    - flexpower_supply_voltage_dc: {min: 10, max: 30} V\n    - flexpower_supply_voltage_ac: {min: 110, max: 230} V\n    - flexpower_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n    - flexpower_supply: \"optional, for SW 5.0.1_ or higher\"\n  - Software versions: 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_; 4.2.5_; 4.2.6_; 4.3.0_; 4.3.1_; 5.0.1_; 5.0.2_; 5.0.3_ — list of strings. This is a list of version strings; store as string value or array? The schema says value can be number | {min,max} | string. A list of software versions — I'll store as a single string with semicolons, or better as an array? Strict shape says value must be number|object|string. I'll store as string \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_, 5.0.1_, 5.0.2_, 5.0.3_\". Hmm, keep the trailing underscore as it's part of the version designation (wildcard for trailing char). Keep it.\n  - Note about field current: \"In case of software version 4.3.1_ or lower the Field Current can only be set to 16 mA. For software version 5.0.1_ or higher the different Field Currents can be selected and shall be set to 16 mA.\" Could be config_level: attribute field_current, axis software_version: condition \"≤ 4.3.1_\" → 16 mA (only), condition \"≥ 5.0.1_\" → 16 mA (selectable, shall be set). Might be noise. I could add a note in matrix or config_level. I'll add config_level entry: attribute \"field_current\", axis \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"}, {condition: \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"}]. That's reasonable.\n\n**Page 3 table — Meter sizes and flow rates:**\n\nThis is a complex table with DN sizes and multiple rows per size (different Q1 variants and accuracy classes). Let me parse carefully.\n\nHeader: Meter size | Accuracy class | Min. Q1 | Trans. Q2 | Perm. Q3 | Over. Q4 | Ratio Q3/Q1. Flow rates in m³/h.\n\nRows:\n1. DN25, class 2 (rowspan 2), Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400 (rowspan 2)\n2. (DN25), Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400\n3. DN40, class 2, Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.1, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, ratio 400\n4. (DN40), Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n5. DN50, class 2, Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n6. (DN50), Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n7. DN65, class 2 (rowspan 2): Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.25, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n8. (DN65, class 2): Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n9. (DN65, class 1): Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250\n10. DN80, class 2 (rowspan 2): Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n11. (DN80, class 2): Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n12. DN80, class 1 (rowspan 2): Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 160\n13. (DN80, class 1): Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250\n14. DN100, class 2 (rowspan 2): Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n15. (DN100, class 2): Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n16. DN100, class 1 (rowspan 2): Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160\n17. (DN100, class 1): Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n18. DN125, class 2 (rowspan 2): Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n19. (DN125, class 2): Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n20. DN125, class 1 (rowspan 2): Q1=1.56, Q2=2.5, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 160\n21. (DN125, class 1): Q1=1.6, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 250\n22. DN150, class 2 (rowspan 2): Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n23. (DN150, class 2): Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n24. DN150, class 1 (rowspan 2): Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 160\n25. (DN150, class 1): Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250\n26. DN200, class 2: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n27. DN200, class 1: Q1=3.94, Q2=6.3, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 160\n28. DN250, class 2: Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 400\n29. DN250, class 1: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 160\n30. DN300, class 2: Q1=4, Q2=6.4, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 400\n31. DN300, class 1: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160\n32. DN350, class \"1 or 2\": Q1=15.625, Q2=25, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 160\n33. DN400, class \"1 or 2\": Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n34. DN450, class \"1 or 2\": Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n35. DN500, class \"1 or 2\": Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n36. DN600, class \"1 or 2\": Q1=63, Q2=100.8, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 100\n\nNote the HTML rowspan structure. Let me carefully reconstruct:\n\n```\n<tr><td>DN25</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,025</td><td>0,04</td><td>10</td><td>12,5</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,04</td><td>0,064</td><td>16</td><td>20</td></tr>\n```\nSo DN25 has two rows, both class 2, both ratio 400. First row: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5. Second: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20.\n\n```\n<tr><td>DN40</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,0625</td><td>0,1</td><td>25</td><td>31,3</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,1</td><td>0,16</td><td>40</td><td>50</td></tr>\n```\nDN40 row 1: Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.1, Q3=25, Q4=31.3. Row 2: Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50. Class 2, ratio 400.\n\n```\n<tr><td>DN50</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,1</td><td>0,16</td><td>40</td><td>50</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,1575</td><td>0,252</td><td>63</td><td>78,75</td></tr>\n```\nDN50 row 1: Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50; row 2: Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75. Class 2, ratio 400.\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=3>DN65</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,1575</td><td>0,25</td><td>63</td><td>78,75</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,25</td><td>0,4</td><td>100</td><td>125</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>0,4</td><td>0,64</td><td>100</td><td>125</td><td>250</td></tr>\n```\nDN65: two class-2 rows: (0.1575, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400) and (0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400); one class-1 row: (0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=4>DN80</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,25</td><td>0,4</td><td>100</td><td>125</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,4</td><td>0,64</td><td>160</td><td>200</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=2>1</td><td>0,625</td><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>125</td><td>160</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,64</td><td>1,02</td><td>160</td><td>200</td><td>250</td></tr>\n```\nDN80 class 2: (0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400), (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400). DN80 class 1: (0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160), (0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=4>DN100</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,4</td><td>0,64</td><td>160</td><td>200</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0,625</td><td>1</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=2>1</td><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>160</td><td>200</td><td>160</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td><td>250</td></tr>\n```\nDN100 class 2: (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400), (0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400). DN100 class 1: (1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160), (1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=4>DN125</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>0,625</td><td>1</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>400</td><td>500</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=2>1</td><td>1,56</td><td>2,5</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td><td>160</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1,6</td><td>2,56</td><td>400</td><td>500</td><td>250</td></tr>\n```\nDN125 class 2: (0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400), (1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400). DN125 class 1: (1.56, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160), (1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=4>DN150</td><td rowspan=2>2</td><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>400</td><td>500</td><td rowspan=2>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1,575</td><td>2,52</td><td>630</td><td>787,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=2>1</td><td>2,5</td><td>4</td><td>400</td><td>500</td><td>160</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2,52</td><td>4,03</td><td>630</td><td>787,5</td><td>250</td></tr>\n```\nDN150 class 2: (1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400), (1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400). DN150 class 1: (2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160), (2.52, 4.03, 630, 787.5, 250).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=2>DN200</td><td>2</td><td>1,575</td><td>2,52</td><td>630</td><td>787,5</td><td>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>3,94</td><td>6,3</td><td>630</td><td>787,5</td><td>160</td></tr>\n```\nDN200 class 2: (1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400). DN200 class 1: (3.94, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=2>DN250</td><td>2</td><td>2,5</td><td>4</td><td>1000</td><td>1250</td><td>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>6,25</td><td>10</td><td>1000</td><td>1250</td><td>160</td></tr>\n```\nDN250 class 2: (2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400). DN250 class 1: (6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160).\n\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=2>DN300</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>6,4</td><td>1600</td><td>2000</td><td>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>16</td><td>1600</td><td>2000</td><td>160</td></tr>\n```\nDN300 class 2: (4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400). DN300 class 1: (10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160).\n\nDN350: class \"1 or 2\": (15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160).\nDN400: (25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160).\nDN450: (25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160).\nDN500: (39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160).\nDN600: (63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100).\n\nSo the model family is by meter size DN25 through DN600, and within each size there can be multiple variants (different Q1 min values and accuracy classes). The most faithful representation: matrix_tables with rows for each variant, with a variant label.\n\nModel IDs: e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025\", \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.04\", etc. That's 36 rows. Let me assign model_id like \"DN25/Q1min=0.025\" or something simpler: since rows within a size differ by accuracy class and Q1, I'll use model_id like \"DN25 class 2 (Q1 0.025 m³/h)\". Hmm, maybe simpler: \"DN25-2-0.025\"? Let me use a readable scheme: `DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025`. For DN350-600 with \"1 or 2\" class: `DN350 class 1 or 2`.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, attributes varying: q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class, verification_scale_interval (from page 4, varies by size group).\n\nLet me build matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1: \"Flow rates by meter size\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows: 36 rows as parsed above. Values with decimal points.\n\nTable 2: \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {meter_size: \"DN25, DN40 and DN50\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.0001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125 and DN150\", ...0.001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450\", ...0.01}\n- {meter_size: \"DN500 and DN600\", ...0.1}\n\nmodel_family.models: one per distinct variant. I'll create models with model_id per row of table 1 (36 models). Each model's attributes: meter_size, accuracy_class, q values.\n\nActually, maybe better model_id convention: for DN25: \"DN25 (class 2, Q1=0.025 m³/h)\". Let me just use \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025\" — hmm but the parenthesis-free version is cleaner for lookups. I'll use format: `\"DN25 class 2 Q1min=0.025\"`. Hmm. Alternatively keep two models per size for the same class... Let me define model_id as `{meter_size} class {class} Q1={q1}`. For \"1 or 2\" sizes: `{meter_size} class 1 or 2`.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: \n- accuracy_class: values per model (string \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\")\n- q1_minimum: m³/h values\n- q2_transitional: m³/h\n- q3_permanent: m³/h\n- q4_overload: m³/h\n- q3_q1_ratio: dimensionless\n- verification_scale_interval: m³ per model (based on size group)\n\nThat's a lot of entries but faithful. Alternatively keep the matrix tables and only include model_level for key attributes. The rules say \"populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level\" for the R137 example with 3 attributes. For this cert with 36 models, doing all attributes for all models is verbose but correct. I'll include model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, accuracy_class, verification_scale_interval, and maximum_pressure is by size group (config).\n\nHmm, maximum pressure: 16 bar(g) for DN200 and smaller, 10 bar(g) for DN250 and larger. This is config_level with axis \"meter_size\": conditions \"DN200 and smaller\" → 16, \"DN250 and larger\" → 10. unit bar(g). Note bar(g) means gauge pressure. unit_symbol \"bar(g)\"? Unitsdb likely has \"bar\". I'll use unit_symbol \"bar\" and note gauge in the attribute name: maximum_pressure_gauge. Actually \"bar(g)\" is standard notation. I'll use unit_symbol \"bar(g)\" — hmm rule 3 says match canonical Unicode form; bar is in unitsdb likely as \"bar\". I'll put unit_symbol \"bar\" and value includes gauge... no. Let me just do unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"? The example shows \"%vol\" raw symbol allowed when not in unitsdb. bar is in unitsdb. But gauge variant may not be. Safer: attribute \"maximum_pressure\", value 16, unit_symbol \"bar\", and add a note? There's no notes field on values besides footnote_markers. I could add config_level entries with unit \"bar(g)\". I think \"bar(g)\" is acceptable as a raw symbol since it's not exactly the canonical \"bar\". Hmm, the caller looks up unit_symbol in unitsdb; \"bar(g)\" won't match. But \"bar\" loses the gauge info. I'll use \"bar(g)\" to be faithful — the caller can fuzzy match. Actually, let me reconsider: I'll use unit_symbol \"bar(g)\" — faithful representation. Hmm, but rule says \"Match the canonical Unicode form when possible\". bar(g) is not canonical. But losing gauge vs absolute distinction changes meaning. I'll keep \"bar(g)\".\n\nWait — the config_level values shape: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. Per schema item 5: values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. OK.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- min_max_admissible_temperature: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. Label: \"admissible_temperature\" with value range. I'll name it \"min_max_admissible_temperature\"? Better canonical: \"admissible_temperature_range\"? The rules say use snake_case canonical labels; the label itself can encode. I'll use \"admissible_temperature\".\n- indicating_range: 99999999, unit \"m³\". (Note: 99.999.999 with dots as thousands separators → 99999999.)\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- software_versions: string list.\n\nPower supply entries:\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V (from \"Battery 3,6 V\")\n- external_battery_pack_output_voltage: 3.6 V\n- flexpower: optional supply for SW 5.0.1_ or higher; 10…30 V DC or 110…230 V AC / 50–60 Hz.\n  - flexpower_dc_voltage: {min: 10, max: 30} V\n  - flexpower_ac_voltage: {min: 110, max: 230} V\n  - flexpower_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n  - power_supply_type: could combine: \"Battery; external battery pack; FlexPower (optional)\". I'll add power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery; external battery pack; FlexPower (optional for SW 5.0.1_ or higher)\"}.\n\nconfig_level for field_current:\n- attribute: \"field_current\", axis: \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\", note: only settable}, {condition: \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\", note: selectable, shall be set}]. The distinction \"can only be set\" vs \"different field currents can be selected and shall be set\" — I can embed in condition text. Let me phrase conditions: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower (field current can only be set to this value)\" and \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (different field currents can be selected; shall be set to this value)\". Good.\n\nDocumentation folder: \"The construction of the water meter is recorded in the Documentation folder no. 10201-7.\" → test_reports? role \"documentation_file\": id \"10201-7\". Yes, schema has role documentation_file. I'll add {id: \"10201-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nTest reports entries — pages: \"includes 41 pages and 14 annexes\". Schema: id, date, pages, role. pages: 41 (or \"41 pages and 14 annexes\"). I'll put pages: 41 and mention annexes... schema doesn't have annexes field. Don't fabricate beyond schema — I could put pages: 41. The annex count would be lost; acceptable. Or pages as string \"41 pages + 14 annexes\"? Rule 8 says don't fabricate; pages field exists. I'll use numbers: 41, 40, 40, 40, 6, 21. Hmm, maybe I can include annexes info in role? No. Keep pages numeric.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 9\". The number is \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" with revision 9. certificate.number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", and revision noted? Schema doesn't have revision field at certificate level, but revision_history has. I'll set number to \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" (revision 9 appears in revision_history). Could include \"revision 9\" in number — better keep clean. Hmm, maybe number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" and add certificate revision in... there's no field. The revision_history includes revision 9 dated 14 July 2016. Good.\n\nscheme: The certificate is issued under the OIML-CS? In 2016, it's the \"OIML Certificate System\" — scheme A vs B refers to OIML-CS Basic vs MAA. NL1 NMi certs are under the standard OIML CS (Basic system, scheme A). Hmm, do I know? OIML-CS introduced in ~2018 has \"A\" (Basic) and \"B\" (MAA). NMi Certin issues both. This cert from 2016 predates OIML-CS branding; the number format \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" is the classic OIML type evaluation certificate format. I'd say scheme \"A\". But not certain — don't fabricate? The field is optional. I'll omit scheme to be safe. Hmm. Actually, the cert mentions \"The notification of NMi Certin B.V. as Issuing Authority can be verified at www.oiml.org\" — standard CS. No mention of MAA. Omit scheme.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006. The certificate covers R49-1, R49-2, R49-3 (2006 editions). Should I record three recommendation entries? Schema shows single recommendation object. The canonical id \"R49\" with edition 2006. accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]. I'll note parts... schema doesn't allow parts list. Just id R49, edition 2006. Hmm, could I add \"part\": 1? Not in schema. I'll keep id: \"R49\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". page_total: 5. date_issued: \"2016-07-14\" (format? I'll use ISO). The cert says \"14 July 2016\". I'll use \"2016-07-14\".\n\nmodel_family: family_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\". models: 36 entries.\n\nLet me define model_ids. To keep them compact and unique:\n\nDN25: \n- \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025\"\n- \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.04\"\nDN40:\n- \"DN40 class 2 Q1=0.0625\"\n- \"DN40 class 2 Q1=0.1\"\nDN50:\n- \"DN50 class 2 Q1=0.1\"\n- \"DN50 class 2 Q1=0.1575\"\nDN65:\n- \"DN65 class 2 Q1=0.1575\"\n- \"DN65 class 2 Q1=0.25\"\n- \"DN65 class 1 Q1=0.4\"\nDN80:\n- \"DN80 class 2 Q1=0.25\"\n- \"DN80 class 2 Q1=0.4\"\n- \"DN80 class 1 Q1=0.625\"\n- \"DN80 class 1 Q1=0.64\"\nDN100:\n- \"DN100 class 2 Q1=0.4\"\n- \"DN100 class 2 Q1=0.625\"\n- \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1\"\n- \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1\" — wait rows 16 and 17 both have Q1=1! Row 16: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160. Row 17: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250. Same Q1 and Q2, differ in Q3/Q4/ratio. So model_id \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1\" collides. Need more distinguishing: include Q3: \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=160\" and \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=250\".\n\nSimilarly check other collisions: DN80 class 1 rows: (0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160) and (0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250) — Q1 differs, fine. DN125 class 1: (1.56...) and (1.6...) fine. DN150 class 1: (2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160) and (2.52, ...) fine. DN100 class 2: (0.4,...) and (0.625,...) fine.\n\nOnly DN100 class 1 collides. To be uniform, maybe include Q3 in all model ids? That's verbose. Alternative: model_id with Q3 included only... inconsistent ids are ugly but fine. Or use a variant index: \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=160\" and \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=250\" while others just Q1. Hmm, let me just add Q3 to all model ids for consistency? That makes ids long: \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025 Q3=10\". 36 ids. It's verbose but unambiguous and consistent. Alternatively use the smallest distinguishing: I'll include Q1 always and Q3 always. Actually, for cleanliness in the matrix tables I'll have a \"variant\" column too.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe simpler model_id: sequential labels like \"DN25-a\", \"DN25-b\"? Less informative. I think \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025 Q3=10\" style is good. Let me shorten: \"DN25 cl2 Q1=0.025\"? Keep \"class 2\".\n\nActually, for the DN350–600 rows the class is \"1 or 2\" — model_id: \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q1=15.625 Q3=2500\".\n\nVerification scale interval per model (page 4):\n- DN25, DN40, DN50: 0.0001 m³\n- DN65–DN150: 0.001 m³\n- DN200–DN450: 0.01 m³\n- DN500, DN600: 0.1 m³\n\nI'll include model_level verification_scale_interval entries for all 36 models.\n\nmodel_level arrays: accuracy_class (string values), q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, q3_q1_ratio, verification_scale_interval. Each with 36 value objects. That's 7 × 36 = 252 value objects. Long but correct. Alternatively, skip model_level and rely on matrix_tables? The rules strongly encourage model_level. The R137 example populates model_level for the varying attributes. I'll do it but maybe keep q values in model_level and the full raw table in matrix_tables. Yes.\n\nAlso indicating_range per size group — same value 99999999 for all, so type_level indicating_range covers it; the matrix table 2 preserves the grouping for the scale interval.\n\nLet me now also double check \"0,1/50\": min 0.1, max 50 °C. Wait — could it be \"0.1/50\"? Yes, decimal comma. For hot water meters R49 MAT 0.1°C to 50°C — hmm, actually 50°C max suggests hot water capable (temp class T50 covers 0.1–50). The description says cold and hot water. OK: {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n\nPressure: \"16 bar(g) for sizes DN200 and smaller, 10 bar(g) for sizes DN250 and larger\". config_level:\n```\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_pressure\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_size\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN200 and smaller\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN250 and larger\", \"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFootnotes: none present. footnotes: [] or omit. There are no footnote markers. Omit or empty array. I'll include empty array? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit.\n\nIndicating range \"99.999.999\" — dots as thousand separators (Dutch convention). Value 99999999. Yes.\n\nCertificate-level: member_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\nTest report numbers: R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01, NMi-13200159-01, NMi-15200645-01. role: type_evaluation_report for all. dates not given.\n\nDocumentation folder no. 10201-7 → test_reports entry {id: \"10201-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nNow revision_history: revision strings: \"Initial\" (or \"0\"?). The table says \"Initial\". Schema example says string like \"0\". I'll use \"Initial\" as given. Date \"28 March 2012\" → \"2012-03-28\". Changes: \"-\" → null. Others as given.\n\nIssuing authority address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]. phone: \"+31 78 6332332\", email \"certin@nmi.nl\", website \"www.nmi.nl\". person_responsible \"C. Oosterman\", person_title \"Head Certification Board\". oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\". name \"NMi Certin B.V.\".\n\nApplicants: [{name: \"K