 unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: \"u:degree_Celsius\"? Rule says leave unit_id null unless certain. The example in the schema shows \"u:degree_Celsius\" as an example. I'll leave unit_id null for safety? Hmm, \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" I'm not 100% certain of exact unitsdb IDs, so null everywhere. Actually °C → u:degree_Celsius seems given in the example shape. The example in rule 2 shows `\"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"` as the illustrative value. It's plausible the unitsdb ID is u:degree_Celsius. I'll fill in a few obvious ones: u:degree_Celsius, u:volt, u:hertz? I don't know exact unitsdb naming (\"u:volt\" vs \"u:voltage\"...). Safer: null everywhere. The caller will look up unit_symbol. I'll use null for all.\n\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- software_versions: \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_, 5.0.1_, 5.0.2_, 5.0.3_\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- external_battery_pack_output_voltage: 3.6 V\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery; external battery pack; FlexPower (optional for SW 5.0.1_ or higher)\"\n- flexpower_dc_supply_voltage: {min: 10, max: 30} V\n- flexpower_ac_supply_voltage: {min: 110, max: 230} V\n- flexpower_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n\nmodel_level: as planned.\n\nconfig_level: maximum_pressure (axis meter_size), field_current (axis software_version).\n\nmatrix_tables: two tables.\n\nLet me write all 36 rows for table 1 with proper numbers:\n\n1. DN25, 2, 0.025, 0.04, 10, 12.5, 400\n2. DN25, 2, 0.04, 0.064, 16, 20, 400\n3. DN40, 2, 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.3, 400\n4. DN40, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n5. DN50, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n6. DN50, 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n7. DN65, 2, 0.1575, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n8. DN65, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n9. DN65, 1, 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n10. DN80, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n11. DN80, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n12. DN80, 1, 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n13. DN80, 1, 0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250\n14. DN100, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n15. DN100, 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n16. DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n17. DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n18. DN125, 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n19. DN125, 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n20. DN125, 1, 1.56, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160\n21. DN125, 1, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n22. DN150, 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n23. DN150, 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n24. DN150, 1, 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160\n25. DN150, 1, 2.52, 4.03, 630, 787.5, 250\n26. DN200, 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n27. DN200, 1, 3.94, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n28. DN250, 2, 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n29. DN250, 1, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n30. DN300, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n31. DN300, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n32. DN350, 1 or 2, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n33. DN400, 1 or 2, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n34. DN450, 1 or 2, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n35. DN500, 1 or 2, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n36. DN600, 1 or 2, 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nModel ids — include class, Q1, and Q3:\n1. \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.025 Q3=10\"\n2. \"DN25 class 2 Q1=0.04 Q3=16\"\n3. \"DN40 class 2 Q1=0.0625 Q3=25\"\n4. \"DN40 class 2 Q1=0.1 Q3=40\"\n5. \"DN50 class 2 Q1=0.1 Q3=40\"\n6. \"DN50 class 2 Q1=0.1575 Q3=63\"\n7. \"DN65 class 2 Q1=0.1575 Q3=63\"\n8. \"DN65 class 2 Q1=0.25 Q3=100\"\n9. \"DN65 class 1 Q1=0.4 Q3=100\"\n10. \"DN80 class 2 Q1=0.25 Q3=100\"\n11. \"DN80 class 2 Q1=0.4 Q3=160\"\n12. \"DN80 class 1 Q1=0.625 Q3=100\"\n13. \"DN80 class 1 Q1=0.64 Q3=160\"\n14. \"DN100 class 2 Q1=0.4 Q3=160\"\n15. \"DN100 class 2 Q1=0.625 Q3=250\"\n16. \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=160\"\n17. \"DN100 class 1 Q1=1 Q3=250\"\n18. \"DN125 class 2 Q1=0.625 Q3=250\"\n19. \"DN125 class 2 Q1=1 Q3=400\"\n20. \"DN125 class 1 Q1=1.56 Q3=250\"\n21. \"DN125 class 1 Q1=1.6 Q3=400\"\n22. \"DN150 class 2 Q1=1 Q3=400\"\n23. \"DN150 class 2 Q1=1.575 Q3=630\"\n24. \"DN150 class 1 Q1=2.5 Q3=400\"\n25. \"DN150 class 1 Q1=2.52 Q3=630\"\n26. \"DN200 class 2 Q1=1.575 Q3=630\"\n27. \"DN200 class 1 Q1=3.94 Q3=630\"\n28. \"DN250 class 2 Q1=2.5 Q3=1000\"\n29. \"DN250 class 1 Q1=6.25 Q3=1000\"\n30. \"DN300 class 2 Q1=4 Q3=1600\"\n31. \"DN300 class 1 Q1=10 Q3=1600\"\n32. \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q1=15.625 Q3=2500\"\n33. \"DN400 class 1 or 2 Q1=25 Q3=4000\"\n34. \"DN450 class 1 or 2 Q1=25 Q3=4000\"\n35. \"DN500 class 1 or 2 Q1=39.375 Q3=6300\"\n36. \"DN600 class 1 or 2 Q1=63 Q3=6300\"\n\nThese are unique. Good.\n\nVerification scale interval by model:\n- models 1–6 (DN25, DN40, DN50): 0.0001\n- models 7–25 (DN65–DN150): 0.001\n- models 26–34 (DN200–DN450): 0.01\n- models 35–36 (DN500, DN600): 0.1\n\nNow, model_level arrays. Attribute entries:\n\n1. accuracy_class (string values \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\")\n2. minimum_flow_q1 (unit m³/h)\n3. transitional_flow_q2 (m³/h)\n4. permanent_flow_q3 (m³/h)\n5. overload_flow_q4 (m³/h)\n6. q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless → unit_symbol null)\n7. verification_scale_interval (m³)\n\nNames: use q1, q2, q3, q4 canonical? \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\"? I'll use \"q1_minimum_flow\", \"q2_transitional_flow\", \"q3_permanent_flow\", \"q4_overload_flow\", \"q3_q1_ratio\". Consistent with matrix column names: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum_flow, q2_transitional_flow, q3_permanent_flow, q4_overload_flow, q3_q1_ratio.\n\nFor matrix table column names in the R137 example they used \"qmax\" etc. I'll use q1, q2, q3, q4 as column keys: columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"] with column_units [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. Rows use those keys. And model_level attributes use \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\" with labels? Rule 1 canonical labels; q1 is fine as attribute name. Maybe clearer: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\". I'll use \"q1_minimum_flow\" etc.? Hmm. Let me pick: attributes: \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"transitional_flow_q2\", \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"overload_flow_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\". And matrix columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"transitional_flow_q2\", \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"overload_flow_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]. Consistent between both.\n\nDo I include model_level for all 7 attributes × 36 models? That's big JSON but let's do it — faithfulness matters. Actually, to keep the output manageable, I could keep model_level for accuracy_class, flows, ratio, and verification_scale_interval. All are model-varying. Yes include all.\n\nThat's a lot of typing but fine.\n\nmodels[] entries: {\"model_id\": \"...\"} only, or with attributes? The R137 example used only model_id. I can add attributes to each model? Schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put per-model attributes in attributes{} too, but that duplicates model_level. The example left attributes out. I'll include model_id plus maybe attributes {} minimal: I'll include attributes with meter_size and accuracy_class for convenience? To avoid duplication bloat, I'll just do model_id entries. Hmm, the schema allows attributes{}. I'll keep models lean: model_id only. The full data lives in matrix_tables + model_level.\n\nNow certificate.scheme — omit.\n\nproject_number: \"SO16202611\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2016-07-14\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nLet me now also consider \"Category\" — \"Water meter\" is in the allowed category examples (\"Gas meter\" etc.; water meter fits).\n\nRecommendation: The certificate references R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. Single recommendation object: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]}. Good.\n\nShould accuracy classes appear in recommendation? Schema has accuracy_classes in recommendation. Yes.\n\nTest reports with pages:\n- {id: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01\", pages: 41, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01\", pages: 40, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\", pages: 40, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", pages: 40, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-1320015